{"name":"Deep South","hex":"#b4989f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Deep South","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A group of cultural and geographic subregions in the American South, typically consisting of Georgia, Florida, South Carolina, Alabama, Mississippi, Louisiana, and Texas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Deep_South"}],"entries":[{"title":"Deep South","blocks":[{"text":"The Deep South or the Lower South is a cultural and geographic subregion of the Southern United States. The term is used to describe the states which were most economically dependent on the plantation system and African chattel slavery, generally Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama, Georgia, and South Carolina. East Texas, North Florida, the Arkansas Delta, South Arkansas, West Tennessee, and the southern part of North Carolina are sometimes included as well."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Map_of_USA_Deep_South.svg/330px-Map_of_USA_Deep_South.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_South"},{"title":"Deep South (Josh Turner album)","blocks":[{"text":"Deep South is the sixth studio album by American country music artist Josh Turner. It was released on March 10, 2017, through MCA Nashville. The album's lead single, \"Lay Low\", was released to radio on September 15, 2014, and reached number 25 on the Country Airplay chart. The second single, \"Hometown Girl\", was released to radio on May 31, 2016. The single peaked at No. 2 on Billboard's Country Airplay Chart and No. 1 on Mediabase, making it Turner's fifth No. 1 single. The third single, \"All About You\" was released to radio on May 15, 2017. It is Turner's first release since 2012's Punching Bag."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ad/Turner_deep_south.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_South_(Josh_Turner_album)"},{"title":"South","blocks":[{"text":"South is one of the cardinal directions or compass points. The direction is the opposite of north and is perpendicular to both west and east."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Compass_Rose_English_South.svg/330px-Compass_Rose_English_South.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png/500px-2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png/1280px-2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png/500px-2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Majority-Black Counties in the U.S. as of the 2020 United States Census","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2020_Census_-_Majority-Black_Counties_in_the_United_States.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Slavery_in_the_13_colonies.jpg/500px-Slavery_in_the_13_colonies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Slavery_in_the_13_colonies.jpg/500px-Slavery_in_the_13_colonies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map of the Thirteen Colonies in 1770, showing the number of slaves in each colony[